.TH QGET 1 "2026 September 27" "Qore 3.0" "Qore Programming Language"
.SH NAME
qget \- download files from URLs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qget
[\fIoptions\fP]
\fIURL\fP...
.br
.B qget
[\fIoptions\fP]
\fIconnection\fP[\fI/path\fP]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBqget\fR downloads resources from \fBhttp\fR, \fBhttps\fR, \fBftp\fR, and
\fBftps\fR URLs, from locations with the other schemes supported by the
Qore FileLocationHandler module (such as \fBfile\fR, \fBdata\fR, \fBsftp\fR,
and \fBconn\fR, and schemes registered by other modules), or from named
connections of the Qore connection providers (see
\fBQORE_CONNECTION_PROVIDERS\fR), and saves them to files named after the
last component of the URL path; \fBqget \-\-help\fR lists the supported
schemes.
HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3 are negotiated automatically; the network options
(redirects, TLS, proxies, and the connect and read timeouts) apply to
\fBhttp\fR, \fBhttps\fR, \fBftp\fR, and \fBftps\fR URLs.
.PP
Data is written to a \fIfile\fB.part\fR file that is renamed when the download
is complete, so an incomplete file never has the final name; the data received
by a download that is interrupted, times out, or fails on the network is kept
in the \fB.part\fR file.
HTTP error responses fail a download, and redirects are followed.
.PP
On a terminal, each download is shown on a line of its own with a progress bar,
the percentage and sizes, the current speed, a graph of the speed in the last
seconds, the protocol, and the estimated time remaining, as far as the width
of the terminal allows; a download without progress is shown as stalled.
Terminals that support \fBOSC 9;4\fR sequences also show the progress in the
tab or the taskbar.
When the output is not a terminal, progress lines are written at intervals.
Progress and messages are written to standard error.
.PP
Pressing Ctrl-C cancels the download in progress and keeps the data received
in the \fB.part\fR file; pressing it again exits immediately.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Output
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-O ", " \-\-output =\fIfile\fP
Write the data to \fIfile\fP; \fB\-\fR writes to standard output.
Can only be given for a single URL, except for \fB\-\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-directory\-prefix =\fIdir\fP
Save files in \fIdir\fP, which is created if it does not exist.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-clobber
Fail with exit code 10 if the file exists instead of overwriting it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-part
Write directly to the file instead of a \fB.part\fR file.
.TP
.B \-\-content\-disposition
Use the file name given by the server in a \fBContent-Disposition\fR header;
only its last path component is used.
.SS Request
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-header =\fI"name: value"\fP
Add a request header (repeatable).
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-user\-agent =\fIagent\fP
Set the \fBUser-Agent\fR header.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user =\fIuser:password\fP
Credentials for HTTP Basic authentication or for FTP.
.TP
.B \-\-compressed
Request a compressed response and decompress it.
.TP
.BR \-\-max\-redirs =\fIn\fP
Follow at most \fIn\fP redirects (default 20); with 0 a redirect fails the
download.
.TP
.B \-\-fail\-with\-body
Save the body of an HTTP error response; the download still fails.
.TP
.BR \-\-http1.1 ", " \-\-http2 ", " \-\-http3
Use the given HTTP version.
.SS Connection
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-proxy =\fIurl\fP
Use the given HTTP proxy.
By default, the proxy is taken from the \fBhttp_proxy\fR, \fBhttps_proxy\fR,
and \fBno_proxy\fR environment variables.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-proxy
Ignore the proxy environment variables.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-insecure
Do not verify server certificates.
.TP
.BR \-\-cacert =\fIfile\fP
Verify server certificates with the CA certificates in \fIfile\fP.
.TP
.BR \-\-capath =\fIdir\fP
Verify server certificates with the CA certificates in \fIdir\fP.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-cert =\fIfile\fP
Client certificate in PEM format.
.TP
.BR \-\-key =\fIfile\fP
Private key of the client certificate in PEM format; the certificate file is
used if not given.
.TP
.BR \-\-connect\-timeout =\fIseconds\fP
Maximum time to establish a connection (default 30).
.TP
.BR \-\-read\-timeout =\fIseconds\fP
Maximum time to wait for data (default 120).
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-max\-time =\fIseconds\fP
Maximum time for each download.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-timeout =\fIseconds\fP
Set the connect and read timeouts.
.SS Display
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Show no progress or messages other than errors; \fB\-qq\fR shows no errors
either.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-silent
Same as \fB\-qq\fR.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show the protocol, status, content type, and size of each response.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Do not use colors; colors are also not used when the \fBNO_COLOR\fR
environment variable is set or standard error is not a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-full\-exceptions
Show full exception information on errors.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help text and exit.
.SS Deprecated options
.TP
.BR \-P " \fIurl\fP"
Same as \fB\-\-proxy\fR; will be removed.
.TP
.B \-e
Same as \fB\-\-full\-exceptions\fR; will be removed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All downloads succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
Another error.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments or URL.
.TP
.B 3
A file could not be written.
.TP
.B 4
Network error.
.TP
.B 5
TLS error, such as a certificate that could not be verified.
.TP
.B 6
Authentication error.
.TP
.B 7
Protocol error, such as too many redirects.
.TP
.B 8
The server returned an error response.
.TP
.B 10
The file exists and \fB\-\-no\-clobber\fR was given.
.TP
.B 11
A time limit was exceeded.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted with Ctrl-C.
.PP
When several URLs are downloaded, the lowest exit code of the downloads that
failed is returned.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR http_proxy ", " https_proxy ", " no_proxy
The proxy for HTTP and HTTPS URLs, and hosts that are reached directly.
.TP
.B QORE_CONNECTION_PROVIDERS
The connection provider modules that resolve connection names.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables colors when set.
.SH EXAMPLES
Download a file:
.RS
.B qget https://example.com/file.iso
.RE
.PP
Download several files to a directory:
.RS
.B qget \-\-directory\-prefix=downloads https://example.com/a.tar.gz https://example.com/b.tar.gz
.RE
.PP
Write a file to standard output through a proxy:
.RS
.B qget \-x http://proxy:8080 \-o \- https://example.com/data.json
.RE
